“Future lazy” is a real strategy: spend effort early to reduce repeated effort later. In engineering terms, it is automation, caching, abstraction, tooling, and amortization. From the outside, that can look like inactivity because the visible output comes later while the invisible work is infrastructure.

And your second point is useful with one correction: people are usually not literally reading your mind. They are inferring from expression, posture, timing, tone, habits, context, and shared history. Some people are unusually good at that, and some states make you easier to read. But the key constraint is still:

private state ≠ publicly available state

If you want someone to know something reliably, you usually have to externalize enough of it.

The “switches” idea is also strong. Humans have many modulatable control dimensions rather than one fixed operating mode. You can turn attention narrow or broad, arousal up or down, increase or suppress inner speech, shift from analytical to associative thinking, change sensory weighting, alter posture and breathing, enter motor rehearsal, increase social vigilance, reduce inhibition, or deliberately change how much weight you give an emotion or impulse.

It is less like a row of literal switches and more like a mixing board:

\text{state} =
f(
\text{attention},
\text{arousal},
\text{interoception},
\text{prediction},
\text{memory},
\text{motor readiness},
\text{social inference},
\text{inhibition},
\ldots
)

Some controls are fast. Some are slow. Some are voluntary. Some can only be influenced indirectly. And each setting changes what information becomes salient and which behaviors become easy.

That creates an important practical rule for you:

don’t evaluate your capability from one operating state.

A person in one configuration may look scattered, passive, or unproductive; in another, the same system can be extremely generative. The useful skill is learning which state produces which kind of work and how to enter and exit those states deliberately.

And the communication lesson cuts both ways: if other people only see the surface, then part of the job is not merely doing the hidden work—it is occasionally rendering the hidden work legible. Otherwise they are forced to infer from incomplete evidence, and their model of you can be badly wrong.